                <div class="mt-10 prose text-[15px] text-slate-700">
                    <h2>Why Having “0” Credit Is Completely Normal in the US</h2>
                    <p>If you just turned 18, moved to the US, or have always paid cash, you are part of the 26 million Americans the CFPB calls “credit invisible” — plus another 19 million with files too thin to score. It doesn't mean you're bad with money. It just means no bank has reported your behavior to the three bureaus yet.</p>
                    <p>The US system doesn't start you at 0 and move you up. It starts you with <em>no file</em> at Experian, Equifax, and TransUnion. Your first goal isn't to get a perfect score — it's to become scorable. That typically takes one account reporting for 1-3 months.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><b>You need at least 1 account open 6 months and 1 reported in last 6 months</b> to generate a FICO Score — per FICO's own minimum criteria (CFPB).</li>
                        <li>Most lenders pull FICO, not VantageScore. VantageScore can generate faster but FICO is what matters for 90% of US lending decisions (Experian).</li>
                        <li>No SSN? You can still build with ITIN or secured cards that report without SSN. Chime Credit Builder does not require credit check and reports to all 3 bureaus.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h2>The 5 FICO Factors You Must Understand (Weight Matters)</h2>
                    <p>Every move you make should map to these. This is how Equifax and Experian explain FICO Score 8, the most used in the US:</p>
                    <div class="not-prose rounded-[16px] border border-slate-200 overflow-hidden my-6">
                        <div class="grid grid-cols-[60%_40%] bg-slate-900 text-white text-[12px] font-bold tracking-widest px-5 py-3"><span>FACTOR</span><span>WEIGHT</span></div>
                        <div class="divide-y divide-slate-100">
                            <div class="grid grid-cols-[60%_40%] px-5 py-3 text-[14px]"><span><b>Payment History</b> — Did you pay on time?</span><span class="font-bold text-emerald-600">35%</span></div>
                            <div class="grid grid-cols-[60%_40%] px-5 py-3 text-[14px] bg-slate-50/50"><span><b>Amounts Owed / Utilization</b> — How much of limit used?</span><span class="font-bold">30%</span></div>
                            <div class="grid grid-cols-[60%_40%] px-5 py-3 text-[14px]"><span><b>Length of History</b> — Age of oldest + average</span><span>15%</span></div>
                            <div class="grid grid-cols-[60%_40%] px-5 py-3 text-[14px] bg-slate-50/50"><span><b>Credit Mix</b> — Revolving + installment</span><span>10%</span></div>
                            <div class="grid grid-cols-[60%_40%] px-5 py-3 text-[14px]"><span><b>New Credit</b> — Inquiries + new accounts</span><span>10%</span></div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <p>When you start at zero, you have no payment history and no mix. That's why the best starter strategy is <b>one revolving account (secured or Chime) + one small installment (Self)</b>. That covers 4 of 5 factors from day one.</p>

                    <h2>5 Proven Steps: Zero to Scorable to 700</h2>
                    <h3>Step 1: Open a reporting account that can't hurt you</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><b>Chime Credit Builder Visa® Secured Credit Card:</b> No credit check, no annual fee, no interest. You move money from Chime Checking to secure your limit, spend, and Chime auto-pays. Reports to Experian, Equifax, TransUnion as revolving. Ideal first card in the US because it prevents high utilization — you set limit yourself (Source: Chime, Experian).</li>
                        <li><b>Alternative if no bank account:</b> Secured cards like Discover it® Secured or Capital One Platinum Secured — require $200 deposit refundable later. Also report to 3 bureaus.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <h3>Step 2: Add an installment builder</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><b>Self Credit Builder Loan:</b> You make payments to a CD that you get at end. Self reports to all 3 bureaus as installment loan. $25/mo for 24 months = $520 savings + 24 on-time marks. No hard inquiry. Requires SSN or ITIN (Source: Self, CFPB method for credit-builder loans).</li>
                        <li>Why it matters: FICO gives +20-40 points for having credit mix. One card + one loan is the fastest legal way to “mix” when starting from zero (Equifax).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <h3>Step 3: Automate to perfection for 6 months</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Set autopay for at least the minimum. <b>One 30-day late can drop a new score 60-110 points</b> (Experian data).</li>
                        <li>Keep utilization under 10%. With Chime, your reported limit = money moved. Keep balance low; Chime reports $0 or small amount.</li>
                        <li>Do not apply for 3 cards at once. Each inquiry stays 2 years.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <h3>Step 4: Grow your file with Experian Boost (optional) + rent reporting</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Experian Boost can add on-time utilities, Netflix, rent (via Boom, RentReporters) to Experian only. Helps if you're thin but not for FICO 8 at all bureaus.</li>
                        <li>Only add if rent already on-time and landlord reports.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <h3>Step 5: graduate and audit</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>At month 7+, check free Experian file via annualcreditreport.com. Dispute errors same day — CFPB says 1 in 5 have errors.</li>
                        <li>Ask secured card issuer to graduate to unsecured and refund deposit. Then consider entry unsecured card.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h2>How Self and Chime Actually Create History</h2>
                    <p><b>Chime (revolving):</b> Unlike traditional secured cards, Chime doesn't check credit and doesn't charge interest. You decide your secured limit by transferring from Chime Spending Account. Each purchase reduces secured amount; Chime uses Secured Account balance to pay by due date. Reports as “paid as agreed” — that's 35% of FICO. Because you set limit, you avoid accidental 80% utilization trap.</p>
                    <p><b>Self (installment):</b> Self holds your loan proceeds in a locked CD at partner banks (Lead Bank, Sunrise). You pay monthly; each payment reported as installment loan payment. At end, you get savings minus fees + interest. This creates installment history (mix) and shows ability to handle fixed payments — key for auto/mortgage underwriting in US.</p>
                    <p>Together: Month 1 = 2 accounts on file. Month 6 = 12 payment events. Month 12 = 24 events. That's enough for many lenders to approve first apartment/used car in the US market.</p>

                    <h2>Realistic Timeline: From 0 to 700 in the US</h2>
                    <div class="not-prose rounded-[16px] border border-slate-200 overflow-hidden my-6">
                        <div class="divide-y divide-slate-100 text-[14px]">
                            <div class="flex gap-4 px-5 py-4"><b class="w-28 shrink-0 text-slate-900">Month 0-1</b><span class="text-slate-600">Open Chime + Self. No score yet — file building. Average US beginner opens 1.7 accounts.</span></div>
                            <div class="flex gap-4 px-5 py-4 bg-slate-50/50"><b class="w-28 shrink-0">Month 1-3</b><span class="text-slate-600">VantageScore appears (~600-650). FICO still “n/a” until 6 months per FICO minimum scoring criteria (CFPB).</span></div>
                            <div class="flex gap-4 px-5 py-4"><b class="w-28 shrink-0">Month 6</b><span class="text-slate-600">First FICO Score generated — typically 620-680 if 100% on-time, utilization <10% (Experian study of credit builders).</span></div>
                            <div class="flex gap-4 px-5 py-4 bg-emerald-50/50"><b class="w-28 shrink-0 text-emerald-800">Month 12</b><span class="text-slate-600"><b>660-700+ achievable</b> with 2 accounts, no lates, mix present. Equifax says 12 months on-time is threshold for “good”.</span></div>
                            <div class="flex gap-4 px-5 py-4"><b class="w-28 shrink-0">Month 18-24</b><span>700-740+ if you keep utilization low and avoid new inquiries. Self CD matures — you get savings.</span></div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h2>7 Mistakes That Keep Americans at 0</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><b>1. Paying Credit Repair companies upfront:</b> Illegal per CFPB — no company can charge before work done. CROA prohibits it.</li>
                        <li><b>2. Becoming an authorized user of a maxed-out card:</b> Their 90% utilization becomes yours. Only join someone with <10% utilization and 5+ years history.</li>
                        <li><b>3. No credit check loan stores:</b> Payday/title loans in many states do NOT report on-time (only collections). They don't build credit.</li>
                        <li><b>4. Closing first card at month 12:</b> Kills average age and limit. Keep it — no annual fee cards can stay open forever.</li>
                        <li><b>5. Using >30% of limit because “it shows usage”:</b> Myth. FICO has no memory for high utilization bonus. Low wins.</li>
                        <li><b>6. Ignoring all 3 bureaus:</b> Chime reports to 3, Self reports to 3. Some cheap cards report to 1 only — avoid them.</li>
                        <li><b>7. Applying for premium rewards too early:</b> Denial = hard inquiry with no account. Use pre-approval checkers without impact.</li>
                    </ul>

